e trusting\n\t\t// freshness).\n\t\tconst DRIFT_RETRY_DELAYS_MS = [0, 300, 1000, 3000];\n\t\t/**\n\t\t * Host-liveness gate: the probe may only conclude `drifted` once the\n\t\t * host has PROVEN it mounted. NEVER body-childness — the plugin mounts\n\t\t * its own dialogs into body, so its presence proves nothing about the\n\t\t * host. The admissible evidence is host-side only: two probe groups\n\t\t * matched (one alone could be a partial early paint), the composer\n\t\t * fingerprint `data-composer-input` (a stable data attribute that\n\t\t * survives hash drift by design, issue #50), or the documented desktop\n\t\t * contract stamp. If none ever hits, the probe honestly stays\n\t\t * `pending` — \"we don't know\" beats \"your build failed to mount\".\n\t\t */\n\t\tfunction driftProbeLivenessProven(matchedCount) {\n\t\t\ttry {\n\t\t\t\tif (matchedCount >= 2) return true;\n\t\t\t\tif (document.querySelector(\"[data-composer-input]\") !== null) return true;\n\t\t\t\tif (isDesktopShell()) return true;\n\t\t\t} catch {\n\t\t\t\t// a throwing DOM stays undecidable — pending, never drifted\n\t\t\t}\n\t\t\treturn false;\n\t\t}\n\t\tconst MATERIAL_SELECTOR_PROBES = [\n\t\t\t\".bqrRRG_card\",\n\t\t\t// T1 (adversarial review): every group here tests ONLY the host hash — a probe that\n\t\t\t// also matched our own attribute would let the marker's tag satisfy it and silently\n\t\t\t// green-light a host whose hashes have all rolled away. These two groups stay here\n\t\t\t// after 10.13.0 gave both rules a hash-free anchor as well: their continued absence\n\t\t\t// on the shell is a FACT worth reading (`anchors.channelAbsent`), it just stopped\n\t\t\t// being evidence of a lost refinement — which is why it is out of A1's pass/fail\n\t\t\t// string and under `tests/host.frosthooks.test.cjs` instead.\n\t\t\t\".lXshSW_root, ._7yHdaG_panel\",\n\t\t\t\".uV2eYG_root\",\n\t\t\t\".nArs4W_panel, .nArs4W_pane, .nArs4W_paneContent, .nArs4W_workbench, .nArs4W_explorerBody\",\n\t\t\tSIDEBAR_FOOT_SELECTOR + \", \" + SIDEBAR_SETTINGS_SELECTOR + \", \" + SIDEBAR_FOOTER_ACTIONS_SELECTOR,\n\t\t\t\".qDHVXG_fade\",\n\t\t\t// Issue #96/#97/#105 and Roadmap A1: the anchors that are hash-free BY\n\t\t\t// CONSTRUCTION, so a re-roll cannot silently kill them — but the surface can\n\t\t\t// still be renamed or removed (`_fade` → something else, `_footArea` → something\n\t\t\t// else, or the shell dropping its `data-shell-overlay` stamp), and the rules that\n\t\t\t// read them would go inert exactly the way `.qDHVXG_fade` went inert unnoticed.\n\t\t\t// These groups are what the classifier's attribute branch exists for.\n\t\t\tAPP_FRAME_SELECTOR,\n\t\t\tSESSION_FADE_SELECTOR\n\t\t];\n\t\t/**\n\t\t * J1 (adversarial review 10.8.0): three of the groups above are anchored on\n\t\t * hashes that scripts/data/dead-hashes.cjs records as 0-hit on the hosts this\n\t\t * repository can measure (`bqrRRG`, `nArs4W`, `qDHVXG`). They are kept as\n\t\t * OR-fallbacks for the `0.1.0-rc.6 ~ 0.1.x` line the README still claims, and\n\t\t * that line is an ASSUMPTION — there is no 0.1.x corpus here. So on every\n\t\t * healthy modern host these three can only ever be missing, which meant\n\t\t * `anchors.drifted` was permanently non-empty while `desktop-support.md`\n\t\t * teaches tooling that `drifted: [] && pending: false` is the ONLY positive\n\t\t * \"all refinements live\" signal — a contract no real host could satisfy, i.e.\n\t\t * a red light that never turns green is as useless as one that never turns red.\n\t\t * They are therefore reported in their own pool. `tests/hashes.test.cjs` pins\n\t\t * this list against the dead-hash registry, so a hash retired or revived there\n\t\t * reddens here instead of drifting silently.\n\t\t */\n\t\tconst RETIRED_PROBE_GROUPS = [\n\t\t\t\".bqrRRG_card\",\n\t\t\t\".nArs4W_panel, .nArs4W_pane, .nArs4W_paneContent, .nArs4W_workbench, .nArs4W_explorerBody\",\n\t\t\t\".qDHVXG_fade\"\n\t\t];\n\t\t/**\n\t\t * Roadmap A1: groups this repository has MEASURED as present on the npm channel and\n\t\t * absent from the official desktop shell — the two channels re-roll their CSS-module\n\t\t * hashes independently, so these can only ever be missing on one of them. They are NOT\n\t\t * retired anchors kept for an unmeasured legacy line; each one names the module path and\n\t\t * the successor base the shell draws for it, and `tests/hashes.test.cjs` re-derives that\n\t\t * claim from the shell's own bytes rather than trusting the literal below. A group leaves\n\t\t * this list the moment either half of the measurement stops holding, which is why the\n\t\t * literal is pinned against `scripts/data/dead-hashes.cjs` in both directions: an entry\n\t\t * nobody probes is a permission, and a probe with no entry is a permanent drift alarm.\n\t\t *\n\t\t * 10.13.0 adds the third direction. Both entries now also carry a hash-free anchor\n\t\t * inside the same selector list (`data-testid=\"todo-panel\"`,\n\t\t * `[data-queue-dock] > [class$=\"_panel\"]`, our own composer marker), measured on BOTH\n\t\t * channels' shipped bytes by `tests/host.frosthooks.test.cjs`. So this pool stays:\n\t\t * it is the runtime's honest statement that the CLASS NAME differs per channel. What\n\t\t * changed is what A1's acceptance reads — see the roadmap item and\n\t\t * `docs/desktop-support.md`'s 「消费判读」: `channelAbsent` is no longer part of the\n\t\t * pass/fail string, because a field that every entry can talk its way out of is a\n\t\t * permission, and the guard that replaced it is static, runs on CI with no host at\n\t\t * all, and asks a sharper question: does a hash-free branch of the SAME rule exist on\n\t\t * both channels, measured from their bytes?\n\t\t */\n\t\tconst CHANNEL_DIVERGENT_PROBE_GROUPS = [\n\t\t\t\".lXshSW_root, ._7yHdaG_panel\",\n\t\t\t\".uV2eYG_root\"\n\t\t];\n\t\tfunction warnOnMaterialSelectorDrift() {\n\t\t\t// A-1 (external review, 9.26.x): sample once was wrong — an\n\t\t\t// async-mounted settings/workbench